{
  "consensus": [
    "Texas law governs (Austin employment / Texas lease)",
    "Substantial detriment element is met ($40k Seattle home loss plus lease/moving costs)",
    "The May 1 written offer with bolded 'supersedes all prior verbal representations' integration clause is a serious obstacle to enforcing the verbal remote-work promise",
    "Promissory estoppel in Texas employment is generally viewed as a 'shield not a sword' and is ill-suited to compel specific performance of an ongoing arrangement; remedy is largely limited to out-of-pocket reliance damages",
    "Manager's actual authority to modify standard terms is absent per HR records, making apparent authority the only viable pathway for Hayes",
    "Hayes cannot obtain summary judgment compelling continued full-remote work; at best he secures reliance damages"
  ],
  "contradictions": [
    {
      "issue": "Summary judgment outcome",
      "views": {
        "panel_1": "Company likely wins SJ on contract (~75-85%); promissory estoppel survives (~40-55%); Hayes may obtain partial SJ on reliance damages",
        "panel_2": "Company's SJ DENIED on reliance-damages theory via Hernandez; enforcement of remote work itself DENIED",
        "panel_3": "Company's SJ should be GRANTED; majority rule (Collins/Leach/Lotito) bars estoppel as sword in at-will employment"
      }
    },
    {
      "issue": "Reasonable reliance",
      "views": {
        "panel_1": "Split: pre-June reliance unreasonable as matter of law; post-June reliance creates triable issue",
        "panel_2": "Foreseeability conclusive for Hayes; reasonableness is fact question not resolved as matter of law",
        "panel_3": "Fails as matter of law because detriment (May 15 lease, home sale) post-dated the contradictory May 1 writing"
      }
    },
    {
      "issue": "Apparent authority over manager",
      "views": {
        "panel_1": "Triable issue; company acquiescence (no objection April-June) and comparator engineers' written approvals create fact question",
        "panel_2": "Triable issue under Elliott v. Great National Life; hiring manager fits profile of agent with at least some evidence of authority",
        "panel_3": "Fails as matter of law; comparator engineers actually undercut Hayes by showing written approval was required; June email is self-bootstrapping"
      }
    },
    {
      "issue": "Weight of merger clause",
      "views": {
        "panel_1": "Dispositive on contract claim but subject to fraud-in-the-inducement exception under Italian Cowboy/Schlumberger",
        "panel_2": "Standard integration clause, not a non-reliance disclaimer; does not extinguish estoppel theory as matter of law",
        "panel_3": "Effectively bars parol evidence and the estoppel claim; Hayes would need to re-plead fraudulent inducement"
      }
    }
  ],
  "partial_coverage": {
    "panel_1": "Most thorough on procedural strategy, probability estimates, parallel doctrines (ratification, waiver, fraudulent inducement pathway), and counsel-level risk allocation",
    "panel_2": "Most developed on direct remediation and Hernandez v. UPS as controlling analog for partial SJ on relocation reliance damages; pre-hire inducement case law (Germain, Lockhart)",
    "panel_3": "Strongest on at-will modification doctrine (Hathaway), shield-not-sword jurisdictional split, and forum-specific caveat about Third Court of Appeals (Travis County)"
  },
  "unique_insights": [
    "Panel 1: Empirical-style probability estimates (~75-85%, ~40-55%, ~25-40%) useful for client counseling; recommends pleading fraudulent inducement in alternative",
    "Panel 2: Distinguishes between standard integration clause and the stricter 'clear and unequivocal' non-reliance disclaimer required to disclaim reliance under Schlumberger/Italian Cowboy",
    "Panel 3: Forum sensitivity (Travis County is in Third Court of Appeals, which has not squarely decided shield-not-sword issue); Roberts v. Geosource treated as 'harshly criticized' outlier rather than controlling",
    "Panel 1: Company's two-month acquiescence in remote work supports ratification theory distinct from apparent authority",
    "Panel 2: Specific enforcement sought is exactly what Texas's shield-not-sword rule forbids; remedy re-characterization is the doctrinal pivot",
    "Panel 3: Collins/Leach line imposes a 'promise to execute a presently existing writing' requirement that oral at-will terms cannot satisfy"
  ],
  "blind_spots": [
    "All three underweight the practical likelihood that a Texas court will treat the 'hybrid schedule per company policy' language as incorporating the July 1 policy by reference, mooting any contract claim independent of estoppel",
    "None adequately address whether Hayes's continued at-will employment after July 1 (without quitting) constitutes acceptance under Hathaway, potentially undercutting his detrimental reliance claim going forward",
    "Limited treatment of evidentiary issues: admissibility of the June email (hearsay, authentication), burden on manager's retirement/no documentation, and possible spoliation concerns",
    "Panel 3 underweights the Italian Cowboy/Schlumberger distinction between merger clauses and non-reliance disclaimers, which Panel 1 and Panel 2 treat as central",
    "Panels 1 and 2 underweight the strict at-will / statute-of-frauds line (Patterson, Collins, Leach) that Panel 3 identifies as dispositive in multiple Texas appellate districts",
    "None address possible contract formation via the June email as a separate written modification (which could itself be barred by the merger clause and authority issues)",
    "Damages discussion is thin on quantification: no panel attempts a coherent measure of lease exposure, mitigation requirements, or whether the $40k home loss is recoverable given the speculative nature of the sale-price differential",
    "None address tax/withholding implications of any damages award or whether reliance damages are taxable as income"
  ],
  "final_guidance": {
    "for_hayes": {
      "primary_theory": "Promissory estoppel for reliance damages only (not specific performance), anchored in Hernandez v. UPS (W.D. Tex. 2007) and Selz v. Friendly Chevrolet",
      "supporting_theories_to_plead_in_alternative": [
        "Fraudulent inducement to circumvent the integration clause under Italian Cowboy/Schlumberger (the bolded clause is a merger clause, not a 'clear and unequivocal' non-reliance disclaimer)",
        "Apparent authority (Elliott v. Great National Life) using the company's April-June acquiescence and the two comparator engineers as manifestations of the principal",
        "Ratification by the company's two-month knowing acceptance of fully remote performance"
      ],
      "critical_procedural_steps": [
        "Plead fraud with particularity (Tex. R. Civ. P. 91a; Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(b)) to preserve the merger-clause exception",
        "Seek partial SJ limited to reliance damages ($40k + lease exposure + moving costs), not a judicial order compelling remote work",
        "Develop the record on apparent authority via discovery into HR's actual practice for remote approvals and the manager's actual hiring authority"
      ],
      "weaknesses_to_address": [
        "Pre-May 1 reliance (home listing decisions) is hard to tie causally to the verbal promise",
        "Timing: detrimental acts on May 15 followed a contradictory writing on May 1 - must argue reliance was reasonable in context of the manager's oral assurances during active negotiations",
        "At-will doctrine (Schroeder, Winters) significantly weakens any expectation of continued remote status"
      ]
    },
    "for_company": {
      "primary_SJ_arguments": [
        "Merger clause is dispositive under Hubacek/El Paso Healthcare/Subaru - oral promises merged into writing cannot be enforced via estoppel",
        "Promissory estoppel is unavailable as a sword in employment under Collins/Leach/Lotito majority rule; oral at-will terms violate the writing requirement",
        "Reasonable reliance fails as a matter of law because detrimental acts post-dated the contradictory May 1 writing with bolded integration clause"
      ],
      "authority_defense": [
        "Manager lacked actual authority (HR records); apparent authority fails because principal's manifestations (written policy, integration clause, written approvals to comparator engineers) affirmatively negate the manager's authority to vary standard terms",
        "The June email cannot bootstrap authority - it is the agent's own statement"
      ],
      "remedy_framing": [
        "Even if estoppel survives, remedy is reliance damages only; specific performance of remote work is not available",
        "Company's July 1 policy is a uniform business rule applied to all engineers, supporting legitimate non-discriminatory motive"
      ]
    },
    "predicted_outcome": "Company prevails on any claim to enforce ongoing full-remote work; the better-than-even chance for Hayes is partial SJ or trial survival limited to ~$40k-$60k in reliance damages under the Hernandez/Selz framework, contingent on the forum (Houston 1st or Waco/Beaumont/Amarillo courts more favorable than Houston 14th per Collins) and the availability of the Italian Cowboy fraud-in-the-inducement exception. The fraudulent-inducement repleading is the highest-leverage litigation move, because the bolded 'supersedes all prior verbal representations' clause, while a merger clause, may not satisfy Schlumberger's 'clear and unequivocal' non-reliance standard and therefore may not bar parol evidence of fraud."
  }
}

